//RS /// Three optional-returning builtins answered the resident tier with a small
//RS /// wrong integer where AOT answered the real value: `last_index_of` gave `0` for
//RS /// `3` (`Examples/features/collections/iter_adapters`), `map.pop(key)` gave `0`
//RS /// for `4`, and `pq.pop()` gave `1` for `9` (`collections/pop_table`). One cause:
//RS /// each host answers with the result-arena Option carrier (`option_i64`, a
//RS /// 1-based `rt.results` handle) while `LowerCtx::uses_result_option_abi` — the one
//RS /// fact every seam reads — did not declare it, so every consumer decoded the
//RS /// packed word `0 = None, bits + 1 = Some` instead. A handle is never zero, so
//RS /// `?? -1` could not fire either, and the printed answer was `handle - 1`: hence
//RS /// `0` for the first optional in a program and `1` for the second.
//RS ///
//RS /// `PriorityQueuePop` is the interesting one. It had an arm until e7fdc84a5 split
//RS /// the `pop` verb into its own op; the lowering and the safety predicate were
//RS /// migrated and this third table was not, so the fix that admitted `pq.pop()` to
//RS /// the resident tier immediately exposed the stale carrier.
//RS ///
//RS /// The absent arm of each is here too, because it is the half a wrong carrier can
//RS /// never get right: a 1-based handle is never zero, so the old decode could not
//RS /// reach `None` at all.
//RS #[test]
//RS fn optional_builtins_agree_on_one_option_carrier_across_tiers() {
//RS     if skip_if_cranelift_host_unsupported() {
//RS         return;
